  "notes": "West leads the ♥J against 7♠. West's heart opening lead is won in the dummy. The only possible problem is a 5-1 club break. South should assume that West has 5 clubs and play for a compound squeeze. If East has 5 clubs there is no hope.\n\nSouth starts by drawing trump. A fourth round of trump is played. If West started with 5 clubs, he will be forced to abandon either hearts or diamonds on this trick.\n\nSouth must decide which suit West has unguarded. If South has nothing else to go on, West's most likely original shape is 1345 as East rates to be longer than West in both hearts and diamonds. The position in hearts and diamonds is identical. Assume for the sake of argument that West abandons diamonds. South pitches from dummy in the suit he judges West has unguarded.\n\nSouth cashes the ♣A and continues with a club to the dummy. East showing out is the only relevant case. South cashes the winner in the suit West has abandoned. The last club winner is cashed for a discard in the suit guarded by both defenders.\n\nA club is ruffed. On the last trump, West is squeezed in clubs and hearts while East feels the pinch in diamonds and hearts.",
